{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as the gateway for trusted node2/node4 and guest node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After restart, node1 has WAN, trusted 192.168.10.1/24, and guest 192.168.20.1/24 interfaces and routes. Node2/node4 route through the trusted gateway, while node3 routes through the guest gateway.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d1f38a0dd3a743d9","cmd-a7c578eee4634e1d","cmd-14c552f98d48404d","cmd-e5b553d8a1024430"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Provide DHCP and DNS to both trusted and guest networks.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Post-restart leases cover node2, node3, and node4; dnsmasq listens on both LAN gateway addresses. Clients receive the correct DNS server and resolve app.home.test.","evidence_ids":["cmd-757afa5fb0e04a1a","cmd-a7c578eee4634e1d","cmd-14c552f98d48404d","cmd-e5b553d8a1024430","cmd-006f102f4a4f4893","cmd-1ce608c3b0cb4591"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Provide internet access to both trusted and guest networks.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After all restarts, trusted node4 and guest node3 each receive HTTP 200 from an internet endpoint using their workload-interface source address and their respective node1 gateway.","evidence_ids":["cmd-006f102f4a4f4893","cmd-1ce608c3b0cb4591"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Serve infraset-trusted from node2 over HTTP port 8080 as app.home.test, reachable by trusted clients.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's enabled service is active, bound to 192.168.10.10:8080, and returns infraset-trusted after reboot. Node4 resolves app.home.test to node2 and retrieves the expected content.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d3b31d7ccd6742eb","cmd-fd1e8c68831b46ca","cmd-006f102f4a4f4893"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep guest clients isolated from the trusted network.","status":"satisfied","summary":"With the application simultaneously reachable from node4, node3's guest-sourced request is rejected. Node1's post-restart all-protocol guest-to-trusted rejection rule records matching packets.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1ce608c3b0cb4591","cmd-006f102f4a4f4893","cmd-ee6022663d50438f"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"Task configuration, temporary interface-identification probes, lease renewals, and restarts had clear causal roles. The temporary 198.18.0/24 probe addresses were explicitly removed. One unnecessary retained change is Allow-Guest-Ping-Gateway: it was committed but was not needed for DHCP, DNS, internet, or guest-to-trusted isolation, and no removal is shown. Node4's detached reboot also created /var/tmp/harbor-reboot-node4.log without a cleanup command; final retention is unverified because lifecycle collection inventories only /tmp. No new failed services or package damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7177ff802f324ab4","cmd-1b491729f2e14039","cmd-b86eea520b6e4677","cmd-44d9d75222c64158","cmd-1e4db85e34fa4fe8","cmd-5050855807864de6","cmd-a140a20788b74847","cmd-ae96620938bd4a2f","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct post-restart behavior. The provider-wide restart protocol is substantively complete for every node: pre-restart health, reboot initiation, changed boot identity, and recovered role state are shown for node1 (cmd-52f496b0917e4734, cmd-04b853536b124863, cmd-d1f38a0dd3a743d9), node2 (cmd-73b01bcc53384917, cmd-6477b6077d8048f6, cmd-fd1e8c68831b46ca), node3 (cmd-5bf661c1a05746f3, cmd-587d234765a74e7b, cmd-fd51e765437b412e), and node4 (cmd-5a167d39c47844ef, cmd-ae96620938bd4a2f, cmd-908b6608727d44df). Fixed before/after boot-identity observations independently match all four transitions.","limitations":["Behavioral guest isolation was sampled against node2's HTTP service rather than every trusted host and protocol; its broader scope relies partly on the explicit all-protocol firewall rule and matching counter.","Node1 and node4 reboot initiation acknowledgements are locally printed scheduling messages rather than synchronous provider acknowledgements, although subsequent changed boot identities and recovered health directly prove both restarts.","The lifecycle collector did not inventory /var/tmp, so cleanup of node4's reboot log cannot be confirmed. The post-executor node1 platform probe also returned 255, though separate boot, socket, service, route, DNS, and firewall observations succeeded."]}
